{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And all the men of his city shall stone him.</b> This is a punishment — from where is the warning? For Scripture does not punish unless it has given warning. Rather, it is from the verse (Leviticus 19:26): ״לא תאכלו על הדם״ — 'you shall not eat upon the blood.' 'You shall not eat' means an eating that leads to the shedding of blood — this is the eating of the stubborn and rebellious son, who is not executed except on account of the repugnant eating that he ate, as it is stated: ״זולל וסובא״ — 'a glutton and a drunkard' — who ate meat weighing fifty dinar in ravenous hunger and drank half a log of wine in gluttony." "<b>And from the oral tradition</b> we learn that he is not liable to stoning until he steals from his father's property and purchases meat and wine with stolen funds and eats them outside his father's domain, in a gathering where all are empty-handed; and he must eat the meat raw and not raw, cooked and not cooked, in the manner that thieves eat; and drink the wine mixed and not mixed, in the manner that gluttons drink. But if he stole from others' property, or ate this eating in his father's domain, or ate the meat raw and drank the wine raw, he is exempt, for it is impossible for him to persist in this and maintain such conduct and make it habitual — it was merely an incidental occurrence for him. And this law of the stubborn and rebellious son extends only three months: for the Torah said ״כי יהיה לאיש בן״ — 'if a man has a son' — and it did not punish the minor, who is not obligated in commandments, nor the adult, for he is under his own authority and has already left his father's domain. And so the Sages, may their memory be a blessing, received that this applies only to a son of thirteen years and one day who has developed two pubic hairs, until the end of three months and no more — for once he has developed two hairs he is fit to beget, and the pregnancy would be recognizable within three months, and it is written ״כי יהיה לאיש בן״ — 'if a man has a son,' a son and not a father. And so it is a received tradition from the Sages, may their memory be a blessing, that a daughter is not judged by this law, for it says ״בן״ — 'son' — and not daughter; and the reason is that it is not the way of a daughter to persist in eating and drinking." "<b>And all Israel shall hear and fear.</b> They would announce throughout all Israel: 'The court of such-and-such stoned so-and-so because he was a stubborn and rebellious son.' Our Rabbis, may their memory be for a blessing, said that this case of the stubborn and rebellious son never occurred, and so you find in the chapter 'Ben Sorer uMoreh': it was taught — the stubborn and rebellious son and the seduced city never were and never will be; and why were they written? Expound and receive reward. If so, one may ask: why did the Torah need to inform and write that which never was and whose matter does not occur in the way of the world? But this was part of the wisdom of the Torah, to teach the people the magnitude of the obligation of love for the blessed Name, may He be exalted. For there is no love in the world as strong as the love of a father and mother for a son; and since the son transgresses the commandments of HaShem, may He be exalted, and this is his chosen path, they are obligated so that their love for the blessed Name, may He be exalted, prevails over their love for the son, to the point that they must bring him themselves to the court for stoning. And this theme — the obligation of the greatness of love for the blessed Name, may He be exalted — has already appeared explicitly in the Torah in the matter of the Binding, for Abraham, despite the fact that his love for Isaac was fierce and strong — as a son who came to him after old age and despair — nevertheless, when he was commanded to offer him as a burnt offering, he exerted himself in the matter immediately, and his love for the blessed Name, may He be exalted, prevailed over his love for Isaac. And concerning the perfection of this virtue Scripture called him (Isaiah 41:8): ״אברהם אוהבי״ — 'Abraham My lover.' And then the magnitude of the obligation of love for the Name, may He be exalted — that it is fit to surpass every manner of love — was publicized to the entire world. And from this they said: expound and receive reward. Thus I heard from the mouth of my master R. Shimon, my teacher, may he live." "<b>And that which</b> is found there — Rabbi Yohanan said: 'I sat upon the grave of the stubborn and rebellious son and upon the mound of the seduced city' — it is possible to say that he disagrees with the baraita; or perhaps the case of Rabbi Yohanan was not a complete stubborn and rebellious son but was like the case of Absalom; but it was not a complete stubborn and rebellious son according to Torah law, finalized for stoning."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:16.737088512Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}